\paragraph{Gumbel vs.\ STE.}
Replacing Gumbel-Sigmoid with STE~\cite{bengio2013ste} degrades PPL from 11.79 to 15.77 (+3.98). % exp_id: ste_baseline_s42

\paragraph{Divergence Direction.}
Forward KL outperforms JSD (+6.4\%) and reverse KL (+8.6\%), consistent with the SNR analysis (\S\ref{sec:method:gradient}); mode-covering ensures no important neuron is omitted, contrasting with reverse-KL generation~\cite{gu2024minillm}. % exp_id: jsd_ablation_s42, reverse_kl_ablation_s42

\paragraph{SPON Contribution.}
Adding SPON~\cite{xu2026spon} bias correction reduces PPL from 12.08 to 11.79 (2.4\% relative; Appendix~\ref{app:spon}). % exp_id: spon_2048_s42, mvp_kl_norm_v2_s42

\paragraph{Loss Function Isolation.}
Removing the penalty from BCE+Gumbel improves PPL (32.22 vs.\ 38.02), partly due to a sparsity gap (38.3\% training vs.\ 50\% target), but remains $2.7\times$ worse than KL (11.79) and worse than vanilla BCE at matched 50\% sparsity (27.97): Gumbel relaxation does not benefit BCE regardless of sparsity control, and KL's advantage stems from the loss function itself. % exp_id: bce_gumbel_no_penalty_s42, bce_gumbel_lagrangian_isolation, eval_bce_v3_s42
The loss effect ($\Delta$PPL=20.43) is $5.1\times$ the relaxation effect ($\Delta$PPL=3.98).

\paragraph{Compensation Redundancy.}
Adding compensation~\cite{gautam2026fastforward} (+67.2M params) to KL provides no benefit at either sparsity level (PPL 8.97 vs.\ 8.96 at 30\%, identical at 50\%; Appendix~\ref{app:comp_convergence}). Compensation networks are designed for fixed-mask inference (DejaVu/FastForward) and do not transfer to TEAL's dynamic global allocation (TEAL eval PPL 44.54 vs.\ per-token PPL 25.44 for BCE+comp). % exp_id: kl_comp_30_s42, kl_comp_30_s456, kl_comp_50_s42, bce_comp_staged_s42

\paragraph{Hyperparameter Robustness.}
Appendix~\ref{app:db_sensitivity} confirms robustness to predictor capacity ($d_b$, $<$3\%), temperature ($<$1.8\%), and training steps (saturated by 800). % exp_id: db_sensitivity_sweep, tau_schedule_sweep, training_steps_sweep


